  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and a few gusty thunderstorms this afternoon \nand early evening.\n\nA mid level shortwave and associated surface trough will cross the \neastern Great Lakes this afternoon and early evening. Moisture \ntransport into the area and convergence along the boundary will be \nenhanced on the nose of a 40 knot low level jet propagating across \nthe eastern Great Lakes late this afternoon and evening. Expect a \nbroken band of showers and a few embedded thunderstorms to cross the \nregion from west to east from mid afternoon through early evening. \nThe fast movement will limit rainfall, with 0.10\" to 0.25\" on \naverage. A few locations may see somewhat higher amounts where \nthunderstorms occur.\n\nSurface dewpoints in the upper 40s to lower 50s along with poor \nlow/mid level lapse rates will keep instability very limited. Even \nwith the modest instability, 40 knots of flow in the boundary layer \nand evaporative cooling supporting quick downdraft development will \nlikely allow for gusty winds in any thunderstorms or heavier \nshowers. While gusty winds are likely, the chance of any severe \ncriteria gusts is very low given the lack of instability.\n\nThe showers and embedded thunder will rapidly end from west to east \nthis evening as the trough moves into eastern NY. This will leave a \nmainly dry overnight, although a few spotty showers cannot be ruled \nout as warm advection increases.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Summer-like heat Monday and Tuesday. \n\nSummer-like heat will build Monday behind a passing warm front \nSunday night. Speaking of which..it is quite possible we could even \nsee a shower or isolated thunderstorm with the passing of this warm \nfront. Have added very low end PoPs Sunday evening and night. \n\nBuilding heights aloft across the eastern 1/3 of the U.S in concert \nwith 850 hPa T's rising to +14 to +17 will support summer-like heat \non Monday across the Lower Lakes. In terms of temperatures, expect \n80s across much of western and north-central New York, with a few \nlowers 90s possible for some of our normally warmest spots. With \nbuilding afternoon instability it will possibly lend to a few \nshowers or an isolated thunderstorm, especially along lake breeze \nboundaries. While it will be warm, closer to the lakes it will be \ncooler given area water temperatures are still not all that warm and \nin the 40s to lower 50s. Take precautions if you plan to visit any \narea waterways next week.\n\nHeat continues Tuesday but we may be a few degrees cooler due in \nlarge part to some clouds moving in as the trough begins to near the \nregion. That said...as the trough nears we may begin to see some \nshowers and thunderstorms in the afternoon-evening. At this \npoint...it appears that the cold front will potentially hold off \npassing through the eastern Great Lakes until late Tuesday night or \neven early Wednesday. This will continue the threat for showers and \nthunderstorms. After the cold front clears the area we will see it \nturn cooler and drier, which will last through the end of the week.",
